Where a crate is offered under more than one license, the one shown is the one
Rumoca selects, in the preference order recorded in `infra/licenses/about.toml`
(Apache-2.0 first, since Rumoca is Apache-2.0). Selecting one of a disjunctive
set is what the license offer permits; the unselected alternatives impose
nothing. The Zenoh ecosystem is `EPL-2.0 OR Apache-2.0` and appears under
Apache-2.0 here; note that it is genuinely in the default build, because
`transport-zenoh` is a default feature of the `rumoca` binary crate even though
it is optional in `rumoca-sim`. Build-only and development-only dependencies
are excluded: they are not in the shipped binary and carry no redistribution
obligation.

A license appears once per distinct copy of its text. Apache-2.0 and MIT copies
differ from crate to crate in the copyright line they carry, and it is the
copyright line that has to survive, so each distinct copy is reproduced rather
than deduplicated to one.

## Weak-copyleft dependency: source availability

`option-ext` is licensed under the Mozilla Public License 2.0 and is reached
transitively through `dirs` -> `dirs-sys` -> `option-ext`. MPL-2.0 is
file-level copyleft: section 3.2 requires that recipients of a binary that
includes MPL-covered code be told how to obtain the source form of that code.
No other dependency of these binaries is under a copyleft license.

The source form of `option-ext`, in the exact version linked into these
binaries, is available from:

- crates.io: <https://crates.io/crates/option-ext>, and for the precise
  version `https://crates.io/api/v1/crates/option-ext/<version>/download`,
  with `<version>` as pinned in `Cargo.lock`
- upstream repository: <https://github.com/soc/option-ext>

Rumoca makes no modifications to `option-ext`. Combining it with Apache-2.0
code is expressly permitted by MPL-2.0 section 3.3.

